{
 "fps": 30,
 "frames": 1786,
 "dur": 59.501,
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "In 1919, a wave of syrup twenty-five feet tall tore through the streets of Boston.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 5.5
  },
  {
   "text": "It was January 15th, and the North End was busy at lunchtime.",
   "s": 5.8,
   "e": 9.599
  },
  {
   "text": "By the harbor stood a giant tank, five stories high, holding two point three million gallons of molasses.",
   "s": 9.899,
   "e": 16.349
  },
  {
   "text": "A rum company used it to make alcohol, racing to beat Prohibition.",
   "s": 16.649,
   "e": 20.712
  },
  {
   "text": "The tank had leaked for years. The company just painted it brown so nobody would notice.",
   "s": 21.012,
   "e": 25.952
  },
  {
   "text": "Then, just after noon, the rivets popped like gunshots.",
   "s": 26.252,
   "e": 29.394
  },
  {
   "text": "The tank split open, and a wall of molasses rushed out at thirty-five miles an hour.",
   "s": 29.694,
   "e": 34.603
  },
  {
   "text": "It knocked a firehouse off its foundation and bent the steel of the elevated railway.",
   "s": 34.903,
   "e": 39.791
  },
  {
   "text": "Twenty-one people died, and a hundred and fifty were hurt.",
   "s": 40.091,
   "e": 43.207
  },
  {
   "text": "Because it was so cold, the molasses set like glue, and rescuers could barely move.",
   "s": 43.507,
   "e": 48.366
  },
  {
   "text": "Sailors from a navy training ship waded in to pull people out.",
   "s": 48.666,
   "e": 52.23
  },
  {
   "text": "The harbor stayed brown until summer.",
   "s": 52.53,
   "e": 54.329
  },
  {
   "text": "And locals say that on hot days, the North End still smells sweet.",
   "s": 54.629,
   "e": 58.201
  }
 ],
